          "distinct": true,
          "id": "4e7325ec1610cc5611abdb9cd98e2e82a43bcfbd",
          "message": "feat(velvet)!: add skew-<n> on both axes and read an important skew (#1441)\n\nSupersedes #1405, carried onto main. Its base branch was replaced by #1433 and #1439.\n\n## What changed\n\n- `skew-<n>`, `-skew-<n>` and `skew-[angle]` now skew both axes, as Tailwind v4 writes them.\n- **Which class wins an axis:** each axis takes the class Tailwind emits last.\n  - Tailwind emits a both-axes skew before a per-axis one (`skew-6`, `-skew-x-12`, `skew-x-12`), so a per-axis class takes its axis whichever was written first. This holds under a variant too: `hover:skew-6 hover:-skew-x-12` gives −12° on x.\n  - A variant payload takes the axis over the base classes.\n  - An important class takes it over every plain one.\n- **Important skews:**\n  - An important skew (`!skew-x-6`, `skew-x-6!`, `!skew-6`, `hover:!skew-x-6`) is now read. It used to be ignored.\n- **Values that are refused:**\n  - A bare value must be a whole number of degrees with no leading zero (`skew-07` gets no rule, as in Tailwind). A bracket is an angle, or a bare `0`.\n  - A both-axes or important bracket the browser rejects (`skew-[10]`, `!skew-x-[5px]`) leaves the element unskewed, like the per-axis brackets already did.\n- **Shadows:** a `shadow-*` on such an element shears with it on both axes. The deviation note for this is removed.\n\n## Docs\n\n- `styling-variants.md`, `styling-arbitrary-lengths.md` and `styling-shadows.md` are updated.\n- Fragments are added under `Changelog~/unreleased` and `Changelog~/breaking`.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01FoK7YxdRjMGmfmRkEEmS88)_",
