          "distinct": true,
          "id": "7a5b8d304499b6a59608385cfb9c53a748cc7026",
          "message": "fix(velvet)!: bail a child its parent hands back the same element, as React does (#1561)\n\nNo issue: a React-parity gap found answering the release gate's survivors in `ComponentRegistry`.\n\n**A child handed back the same element bails, as React does.** React's `beginWork` bails a fiber whose props object is the one it last rendered with (`oldProps === newProps`) and that has no work of its own — with the React Compiler, a cached part hands back the same element, so a child built there keeps its output. Velvet re-rendered every non-memo child on its parent's render. `ComponentRegistry` now bails a component reached with the `ComponentNode` it last rendered from when nothing is pending for it (`FiberPendingWork.HasOwn`: no suspended own pass, no parked reconcile, no pending async read); `IsDirty` and `RenderOwed` still force a render. `ComponentNode` is never pooled, so the same instance is the same element.\n\n**Pending work below a handed-back element still renders.** React descends only along `childLanes`. Each fiber now carries `PendingWorkBelow`, set on its ancestors wherever a fiber gains work of its own (the `IsDirty`, `RenderOwed` and `SuspendedOn` setters, a parked reconcile, a pending `Hooks.Use` read, `AppendChild`) and cleared once a walk finds nothing below. The reconciler's five reference-identical skips (indexed common phase, keyed linear prefix and its resume, matched suffix, keyed Pass 2) skip while the reconciling fiber is unmarked — one bit read on the steady path — and, while it is marked, still skip an element that cannot hold a fiber (`ElementNode.MayHoldFiber`, cached per instance: only host elements and text below it); every other identical node is entered. The memo-bailed owner's reused output in `GeneralPathReconciler.CommitLeaf` asks `FiberPendingWork.Below`.\n\n**The compiler now caches `V.Component(Row.Render)`.** The C# compiler keeps a static method group's delegate in a `<>O` field it writes on first use; the weaver read that as \"writes a static\" and rebuilt every such scope. `ReactiveScopeVolatility.IsDelegateCache` treats `<>O` fields as it already treated lambda caches.\n\n**Outlet.** A route element is a fixed node in the route table, so a non-memo route no longer renders again on an Outlet reconcile at the same location — as React Router 7's `useOutlet` hands back the element `_renderMatches` built. A route scope whose creation failed is therefore asked for again only when the Outlet next renders.\n\nBreaking — a working app's children render less often (one reading a static or `Time.time` shows its last value until its own update) — so the fragment is under `Changelog~/breaking/`; `react-migration.md`'s two-axes note and the `Memoize` docs are rewritten.\n\nTests: `CachedChildNodeBailTests`, `HandedBackSubtreePendingWorkTests` (a keyed reorder under a Suspense, an element handed back over 200/400 fibers that the walk must not enter, the mark clearing), `SuspendWithoutBoundaryTests` cases under a handed-back element and component, a `ReactiveScopeRunEffectTests` method-group case, route-scope and Outlet cases. Fixtures whose helpers need the old re-render use `Uncompiled…` copies with a characterization declaration.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
